acknowledged, its applications expanded beyond the realm of
gambling. Investors and financial theorists began to notice
the parallels between betting and investing, particularly
regarding the management of uncertainty and risk. This
transition from casino tables to financial markets marked the
beginning of the Kelly Criterion's profound influence on
investment strategies and financial theory, setting the stage
for its integration into various facets of risk management and
portfolio optimization.
In summary, the origins of the Kelly Criterion illustrate a
fascinating convergence of gambling, information theory,
and investment strategy. John Kelly's groundbreaking work,
significantly influenced by Claude Shannon's theories, laid
the foundation for a formula that would transform how both
gamblers and investors approach the management of risk and
resource allocation in the face of uncertainty.

