Chapter 3 : From the Casinos to Wall
Street - Practical Applications
The Kelly Criterion's journey from the casinos to Wall Street
is a fascinating tale of how a formula designed for optimal
betting found its place within the financial markets. Initially
conceived to guide bettors in sizing their stakes for repeated
gambling to maximize long-term growth, the Kelly
Criterion's principles were soon recognized for their broader
applicability, especially in investment.
In the world of gambling, where the Kelly Criterion first
proved its worth, bettors quickly realized that the formula
could systematically turn the odds in their favor. However, it
wasn't long before these innovative concepts made their way
into the more sophisticated realm of financial markets.
Traders, investors, and financial engineers began to see the
clear parallels between betting on horse races or sports games
and investing in stocks, options, and other financial
instruments.
One of the early adopters of the Kelly Criterion in the world
of sports betting was Edward O. Thorp, a mathematics
Scan to Download

Scan to Download
professor who went on to become a legendary hedge fund
manager. Thorp used the Kelly Criterion in conjunction with
his card counting techniques to beat the casinos at blackjack,
as documented in his famous book "Beat the Dealer." Seeing
the potential beyond gambling, Thorp transitioned to Wall
Street, applying the Kelly Criterion to his quantitative
investment strategies with tremendous success. His hedge
fund, Princeton/Newport Partners, demonstrated the
practicality of the Kelly approach in delivering superior
risk-adjusted returns over an extended period.
Similarly, other financial luminaries saw the value of the
Kelly Criterion. For instance, John L. Kelly Jr.’s formula
found champions in James Simons of Renaissance
Technologies and Warren Buffett of Berkshire Hathaway,
both of whom have, in their ways, acknowledged using
optimal money management strategies akin to the Kelly
Criterion. Simons, a cryptanalyst turned hedge fund tycoon,
founded Renaissance Technologies, which is famous for its
quant-driven Medallion Fund. By employing sophisticated
algorithms and leveraging the principles of the Kelly
Criterion, Simons achieved unparalleled returns, making
Renaissance Technologies one of the most successful hedge
funds in the world.
